hls: a cue time far outside any clock is not unwrapped

nearest_period() (0083) subtracts the reference from a cue's time and adds
half a period: a cue time the server wrote out of all range, or the
INT64_MIN of a rescale that overflowed, makes that arithmetic overflow, so
the result is undefined.

Leave a time at or beyond 2^61 (of either the cue or the reference) as it
is, so the difference and half a period stay in range; a real MPEG-TS
time is 33 bits.

diff --git a/libavformat/hls.c b/libavformat/hls.c
index 6013e2c..7180e03 100644
--- a/libavformat/hls.c
+++ b/libavformat/hls.c
@@ -2418,11 +2418,16 @@ static int64_t media_position(HLSContext *c)
 }
 
 /* The MPEG-TS time of a cue is on the 33 bit PES clock, this is the period
- * of that clock nearest to ref, both in MPEG_TIME_BASE_Q. */
+ * of that clock nearest to ref, both in MPEG_TIME_BASE_Q. A time far outside
+ * any clock (a cue's own bogus time, a failed rescale) is left as it is. */
 static int64_t nearest_period(int64_t pts, int64_t ref)
 {
-    const int64_t period = 1LL << 33, half = 1LL << 32;
-    int64_t d = pts - ref;
+    const int64_t period = 1LL << 33, half = 1LL << 32, limit = 1LL << 61;
+    int64_t d;
+
+    if (pts <= -limit || pts >= limit || ref <= -limit || ref >= limit)
+        return pts;
+    d = pts - ref;
 
     return pts - (d >= 0 ? d + half : d - half) / period * period;
 }
